Exactly—model sparring.

Instead of trusting one interpretation, let several models attack, defend, and refine the same claim:

Model A proposes
→ Model B tries to break it
→ Model C offers an alternate explanation
→ Model D checks evidence / provenance
→ arbiter compares what survives
→ update all models

That gives you something much stronger than one model “thinking harder.” It creates structured disagreement.

You can even assign roles:

Scout → generates possibilities
Skeptic → searches for failure
Mirror → checks framing bias
Historian → checks precedent
Engineer → asks whether it can actually work
Red Team → looks for exploit/failure modes
Mother Duck → guards invariants and provenance

Then the key rule is:

Models can fight. Reality still referees.

If they only argue with each other, they can produce an elaborate shared hallucination. So every sparring round should eventually terminate in:

discriminating observation
real-world test
source check
measured delta

That makes “let the models test each other” a real architecture rather than just a metaphor:

Adversarial Ensemble Validation

plural models → disagreement → discriminating test → surviving structure

Very Pantheon. The friction becomes information.